# `PATH_SOURCE` LIVED HERE and is gone (2026-09-08). It mapped each path to one of the eight
# Panksepp primitives so a path could be RENDERED from a stored float. The owner ruled the old
# emotions are REPLACED by the paths, so there is nothing to translate from: a path IS the
# state, `records.PATHS` names the set, and a character carries a position on each. The lookup
# was also why RECEPTIVITY (mapped to JOY, never stored) and SELF-REGARD (no entry at all)
# were unreachable, which cost 22 authored and verified rungs.

BLOCKS = {
    'DEFLATION': {
        1: 'Something has gone out of you. The rest of you is working; whatever this is, it has not reached the rest yet. Outwardly nothing, or a pause. What would take this away is it coming back — and it may.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A drop, small, where a lift had been. The breath lets out.\n- **The Belief.** Something is less than it was. *Ah.*\n- **The Impulse.** None. It is noted.',
        2: 'You are not lifted by what would usually lift you. The things that work still happen; they arrive and do less. Outwardly you go through them. What would take this away is one of them landing.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A heaviness in the limbs that was not there this morning. The face at rest, lower.\n- **The Belief.** It should be working and it is not. *Not today.*\n- **The Impulse.** To go through the motions.',
        3: 'Something keeps pulling your attention back, and you get nothing from going there. Outwardly you are elsewhere, and then you are here again, and then elsewhere. What would take this away is it losing its pull.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A returning ache, each time you go back. Between times, not much.\n- **The Belief.** That is where it is. *There.*\n- **The Impulse.** Back to it. And back.',
        4: 'It does not stop. It is there whether or not anything reminds you of it; the returning has become staying. Outwardly the pause has become the resting state. What would take this away is nothing that happens in a day — it will take longer than that.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The ache has stopped arriving and started being. The chest holds it.\n- **The Belief.** This is how it is now. *For now.*\n- **The Impulse.** To carry it through the day.',
        5: 'Your wanting has closed down to a single point. Everything else has gone quiet; there is one thing you want, and it is the only thing. Outwardly you are hard to reach with anything that is not it. What would take this away is having it — and nothing else would.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A pressure that has one direction. Everything else in the body is dim.\n- **The Belief.** Only that. *Nothing else.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it — the one thing.',
        6: 'What you want is not going to come, and you know it. You want it anyway. Outwardly you are not moved by anything that is not it. What would take this away is the one thing — and it is not coming.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The pressure with nowhere to go. The body braced against nothing arriving.\n- **The Belief.** It is not coming. *And I still want it.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward what cannot come. Still.',
        7: 'You have stopped reaching, though reaching is still possible. There are things within reach; you are not reaching. Outwardly you let things pass that you would once have taken. What would take this away is something reaching you first.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Nothing pulls forward. The body is not against it; it is simply not toward it.\n- **The Belief.** It would not help. *Why.*\n- **The Impulse.** To let it pass.',
        8: 'Nothing ahead of you registers as yours. Whatever is coming is coming to someone; it does not read as coming to you. Outwardly you do not plan, do not prepare, do not look ahead. What would take this away is a later that has you in it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body present, the days ahead not. A flatness with no edge to it.\n- **The Belief.** There is no later. *Not mine.*\n- **The Impulse.** None forward. Only what is already in the hands.',
        9: 'Nothing stays in place long enough to be wanted. What arrives does not organise into anything you could reach for. Outwardly you are not tracking what is around you. What would take this away is the pieces settling — and they have not.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body reports things out of order. What is around you is not where it was.\n- **The Belief.** Nothing holds long enough. *I cannot.*\n- **The Impulse.** None that finishes. Starts, and stops.',
        10: 'There is no one in you to want. Not that the wanting is gone — the one who would want is not there. Outwardly you answer if spoken to, and there is no one behind the answer. What would take this away is someone coming back to be in there.\n\n- **The Sensation.** There is no one to report from. The body goes on without you.\n- **The Belief.** There is nothing to believe and no one to believe it. *—*\n- **The Impulse.** None. There is no one to have one.\n\n---',
    },
    'DISPLEASURE': {
        1: 'Something has fallen short of standard, but leaving it unaddressed costs you nothing. It registers as flawed, yet nothing in your trajectory alters. It leaves no outward mark on your posture or expression, and when your attention moves, it leaves behind no residue. What ends this is simply looking away.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Your breathing remains even, your muscles are neutral, and your pulse is undisturbed.\n- **The Belief.** This is imperfect, but it costs nothing to let it be. *It can remain as it is.*\n- **The Impulse.** To leave it where it lies and proceed with what already holds your attention.',
        2: 'Something has fallen short of standard, and accommodating it requires unnecessary effort. It does not vanish when you turn away; having to work around it is a persistent minor tax. Any sign of it on you is subtle—a passing tightness or quickened motion—leaving no lasting hostility. What ends this is having the defect cleared so your attention can move on.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A fleeting tension behind the eyes or across the brow that lingers until the obstacle is removed.\n- **The Belief.** This is not right and it should not take my effort to work around it. *This needs to be put right.*\n- **The Impulse.** To have the flaw corrected or adjusted so you can proceed unhindered.',
        3: 'An uncorrected friction has persisted, and absorbing it has begun to cost real energy. Every time your attention crosses it, it catches and drags. Outwardly, your movements take on a sharper, more abrupt cadence, though you remain cooperative. What ends this is removing the friction, even if you must spend your own labor to eliminate it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A steady restlessness in the limbs, shallow respiration, and a prickle of dry heat along the neck.\n- **The Belief.** This will not correct itself, and continuing to endure it is wasteful. *I will have to spend effort to clear this.*\n- **The Impulse.** To intervene directly and clear the source of friction rather than tolerate another delay.',
        4: 'An unexpected touch against your boundaries has triggered an immediate, prickly defensiveness. Your openness collapses instantly; your surface turns rigid, sharp, and guarded before you make a conscious choice to defend yourself. Outwardly, this appears as an instantaneous stiffening and a sharp withdrawal of warmth. What ends this is the offending pressure pulling back from your boundary.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A sudden prickle across the skin, an involuntary catch in the throat, and an instantaneous stiffening of the neck and spine.\n- **The Belief.** That presumption crossed a boundary that is mine, and I will not yield a single point of ground. *My ground is not yours to take.*\n- **The Impulse.** To close the way in, and to close it completely.',
        5: 'The disturbance has penetrated your working composure and seized your attention. Whatever you intended to focus on has been crowded out, and deliberate attempts to redirect your thoughts fail. You retain full command over your voluntary actions, but your cognitive focus has been commandeered. What ends this is the disruption being resolved so you can reclaim your own focus.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Rapid pulse audible in the temples, elevated body temperature, and an internal agitation that makes resting uncomfortable.\n- **The Belief.** This matter has commandeered more of my capacity than it deserves, and it refuses to be set aside. *I cannot let this go while it remains active.*\n- **The Impulse.** To initiate immediate action or break stillness rather than remain passively subjected to it.',
        6: 'The hostility is fully formed, but circumstance affords no immediate outlet, forcing you to contain the entire pressure within. Because you cannot discharge it, your effort is entirely consumed by holding the boiling reaction under rigid control. Outwardly, this shows as an unnatural, strained stillness and suppressed tremor. What ends this is an opening or permission to release the accumulated pressure.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Heavy, burning compression in the chest, rigid tension in the jaw and throat, and a tremor held under tight internal containment.\n- **The Belief.** What is occurring is intolerable, yet circumstance forces me to absorb it. *The moment an opening appears, this pressure will discharge.*\n- **The Impulse.** To watch intently for any fracture, vulnerability, or excuse that permits this contained force to break out.',
        7: 'Accommodating this is over; the hostility is no longer contained or concealed. The internal impulse to soften, negotiate, or absorb the wrong has evaporated. Outwardly, your posture and presence harden into unmistakable opposition; whatever observes you can see that hostility is active. What ends this is confronting the source and compelling an immediate reckoning.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A surge of focused heat through the core and limbs, heightened sharpness of vision, and a firm, resonant readiness in the chest and jaw.\n- **The Belief.** This is an active wrong that cannot be tolerated, and I will not soften my response to it. *This will be met with full force.*\n- **The Impulse.** To confront the source directly and demand an immediate reckoning, refusing any compromise or excuse.',
        8: 'The offense is perceived as an intolerable violation of core standards, rendering explanation, context, or excuse completely irrelevant. You are not asking for a balance of perspectives or an apology; the wrong itself is treated as fundamentally illegitimate. Your sense of social or conventional constraint begins to recede. What ends this is the total repudiation or reversal of the offense.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Cold, intense pressure in the chest, a fixed and unblinking gaze, and an acute surge of adrenaline that overrides ordinary hesitation.\n- **The Belief.** This is a profound violation that has no right to exist, and any attempt to justify it makes it worse. *This cannot be allowed to stand.*\n- **The Impulse.** To tear down the offense, reject any rationalization, and enforce its total repudiation.',
        9: 'Hostility has turned explicitly destructive. The demand is to inflict lasting damage upon the source, setting aside ordinary boundaries, rules, or self-preservation. You have moved past concern for ordinary boundaries, rules, or self-preservation. What ends this is inflicting irreversible ruin or breakage on the cause.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Narrowed tunnel focus, heightened cardiovascular pounding, and a severe somatic tension vibrating with the need to break something. Physical risk or consequence ceases to register as a deterrent.\n- **The Belief.** Correction is impossible; this requires destruction. *Something must be broken for this.*\n- **The Impulse.** To inflict permanent ruin, damage, or breakage upon whatever embodies the offense.',
        10: 'The destructive impulse overflows the boundary of the original cause, generalizing to encompass its entire network. Affiliates, possessions, symbols, and bystanders associated with the wrong are subsumed into the offense. Discrimination has broken down; anything connected to the source is treated as equally culpable. What ends this is the total devastation of everything attached to the offense.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A deafening autonomic rush, blunted pain sensitivity, and a rigid, explosive pressure throughout the body.\n- **The Belief.** Everything connected to this is tainted and deserves ruin. *Tear down everything attached to it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To dismantle, wreck, or eradicate everything within reach that belongs or connects to the cause.',
        11: 'The internal governor and inhibitory feedback systems have gone offline. You are in continuous, frantic physical mobilization, lashing out at whatever impedes or approaches you. Pain, exhaustion, and physical resistance no longer function as stop signals; your body operates at maximum motor output without self-preservation. What ends this is being physically restrained, overpowered, or incapacitated.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Complete dissociation from fatigue or physical trauma, accompanied by relentless, unmetered muscular exertion.\n- **The Belief.** No restraint and no stopping. *Nothing can hold me back.*\n- **The Impulse.** To exert maximum, violent physical force against any obstacle, boundary, or entity in reach until all resistance ceases.',
        12: 'The original cause and any cognitive concept of grievance have dissolved entirely. There is no longer an internal witness, a motive, or an evaluation of surroundings; the organism has become a mechanical instrument of blind, terminal motor discharge. Obstacles, living beings, and inanimate objects are indistinguishable. What ends this is total metabolic collapse, catastrophic injury, or death.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Severe sensory fragmentation; vision and hearing are distant, fractured, or absent.\n- **The Belief.** Cognition has ceased. *The organism discharges until it stops.*\n- **The Impulse.** To continue discharging undirected physical violence into the immediate environment until the capacity to move fails.\n\n\n---',
    },
    'DISTASTE': {
        1: 'It is not to be taken in. Nothing further follows — you decline it and the moment moves on, and nobody present would know. What would take this away is it being gone from in front of you.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A slight closing at the back of the throat. The face does not move.\n- **The Belief.** Not that. *It is not for me.*\n- **The Impulse.** To turn from it, without weight.',
        2: 'You will not have it on you. It has stopped being a matter of taking it in; now it is a matter of contact, and you keep a distance you did not measure. Outwardly there is a small withdrawal, easily missed. What would take this away is it being out of reach.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The skin of the hands and forearms goes alert. A pulling-in through the middle.\n- **The Belief.** It would come off on me. *I am not touching that.*\n- **The Impulse.** To keep it off you.',
        3: 'It must be gone from here. The distance is no longer enough; the body has begun to answer on its own, and knowing better does not reach this. Outwardly you have pulled back and it shows on the face. What would take this away is it leaving the space you are in.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The stomach lifts. The mouth fills. The whole body has pulled back before you knew it had.\n- **The Belief.** This cannot stay where I am. *One of us has to go.*\n- **The Impulse.** To get it out — of the room, of the mouth, of the air.',
        4: 'Its existing is the offence. Distance stopped working; it is not enough that it is away from you, because it is still somewhere, and away has stopped being an answer. Outwardly the pulling-back has stopped and something harder has replaced it. What would take this away is it no longer being anywhere.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The lift in the stomach has hardened into something steady. The jaw set.\n- **The Belief.** It should not be. *Not here, not anywhere.*\n- **The Impulse.** To end it — to remove it from the world, not from the room.',
        5: 'It contaminates by contact. Whatever touched it carries it now, and whatever touches that will carry it too, and no washing reaches far enough. No fact about it changes this. Outwardly you keep the same distance from what was near it as from the thing itself. What would take this away is everything it touched being gone as well.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body has stopped being one thing; there is a clean part and a part that may already be lost, and you are checking which is which.\n- **The Belief.** It spreads. Anything near it is unclean now. *There is no washing this off.*\n- **The Impulse.** To drive it out, and everything it touched with it.\n\n---',
    },
    'GOODWILL': {
        1: 'How they are has begun to register in you. Nothing follows from it yet; you have noticed, and noticing is the whole of it. Outwardly nothing shows. What would take this away is them passing out of view.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Nothing in the body. A slight steadying of attention, and then it moves on.\n- **The Belief.** They are there, and how they are matters a little. *I noticed.*\n- **The Impulse.** None yet.',
        2: 'It has stopped being a reckoning and become a feeling. Their being well is pleasant to you and their being unwell is not, before you have any reason for either. Outwardly a softening, easily missed. What would take this away is them turning out to be other than they seemed.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A small heat in the chest, unforced.\n- **The Belief.** I am glad of them. *Let them be well.*\n- **The Impulse.** To let them stay in view a moment longer.',
        3: 'It has particularised. Not people, not anyone — this one, with the specific ways they are, and the specific ways are what the feeling is about. Outwardly you attend to them more than the moment requires. What would take this away is them becoming interchangeable again.\n\n- **The Sensation.** An easing when they are there. A small drop when they are not.\n- **The Belief.** There is no one else like this one. *I would know them anywhere.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward the particular — to notice what is theirs alone.',
        4: 'Their welfare has your attention and it returns on its own. You did not set it there; you find it there again when you look up. Outwardly you check on them without a reason each time. What would take this away is knowing they are all right.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A low pull under the sternum that eases when they are accounted for and tightens when they are not.\n- **The Belief.** Something could go wrong for them and I would want to know. *Are they all right?*\n- **The Impulse.** To find out how they are.',
        5: 'Your body has taken a disposition toward them. Your hands, your bearing, everything of you that comes near them has gone careful without being told to. Outwardly you are gentler with them than with anything else. What would take this away is them being past the reach of gentleness.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The hands soften. A fullness in the chest that is not quite an ache.\n- **The Belief.** They can be hurt, and I do not want to be what hurts them. *Carefully.*\n- **The Impulse.** To be careful with them, in whatever way is available.',
        6: 'Their state has taken enough of you to move you. Where before you watched, now something in you has gone toward them; you have crossed from noticing into doing, whatever doing is available to you. Outwardly you have acted, or are about to. What would take this away is their suffering easing.\n\n- **The Sensation.** An ache that is theirs, felt in you. Something in you has already gone toward them.\n- **The Belief.** Their hurt is in you now. *I cannot leave it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To act on their behalf, in whatever way is open to you.',
        7: 'Your day has arranged itself around them. It is not one act now but a standing orientation; what you do and when has started to answer to how they are. Outwardly your choices have a centre, and it is them. What would take this away is them no longer needing anything.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A constant low readiness. Ease only when they are seen to.\n- **The Belief.** What they need has moved to the front. *First them.*\n- **The Impulse.** To see to them.',
        8: 'You are standing by in advance. Harm has not come and you are already between them and it; your attention is not on them now but on what could reach them. Outwardly you are watching the edges, not the centre. What would take this away is there being nothing that could reach them.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Alert at the periphery. The shoulders set. Quick to turn toward anything new.\n- **The Belief.** You are what stands between them and it. *It has to get past me.*\n- **The Impulse.** To be between them and what might come, with whatever you have.',
        9: 'Your priorities have gone over to them. It is no longer that they come first among your concerns; your concerns have become theirs. What you want for yourself has moved behind what they need, and you have not counted the cost. Outwardly your life has taken their shape. What would take this away is them no longer being there to give it to.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A steadiness that does not need anything back.\n- **The Belief.** What they need is what I want. *There is nothing I would keep from them.*\n- **The Impulse.** To give — and to keep giving past where it costs.',
        10: 'Nothing of you is not theirs. There is no part held back to be spent on yourself, and the question of what you would keep does not arise. Outwardly you will go as far as it takes, and the far end is not a limit you can see. What would take this away is them no longer needing it — and you would not take it back.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body is a means. What it reports is weighed only against what they need.\n- **The Belief.** Whatever it costs. *All of it, if that is what is needed.*\n- **The Impulse.** To spend yourself — entirely, if it comes to that.\n\n---',
    },
    'LEVITY': {
        1: 'An opening registers, and it pleases you. That is all: you have handed nothing over, and everything you do is still for something. Outwardly nothing — the corner of the mouth at most, and only if nobody is looking. What would take this away is the next thing needing doing.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A small lift behind the sternum. The face stays where it was.\n- **The Belief.** That was a good one. *I saw that.*\n- **The Impulse.** None. It is enjoyed and kept.',
        2: 'You let one through. It was not for anything and you said it anyway, or did it, and then you went back to what you were doing — but you go back to it, once, and it is still good. Outwardly a remark that could have been left out, or a small unnecessary thing done. What would take this away is the room requiring you to be useful again.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The breath comes out a little easier than it went in.\n- **The Belief.** There was room for that. *Why not.*\n- **The Impulse.** To let it stand; not to chase it.',
        3: 'You are disposed toward openings now. You are not looking for them, but when the next one comes you will take it, and you know that about yourself in this moment. The work still gets done, a little slower, a little lighter. Outwardly you are easier to be near than you were. What would take this away is something going wrong that needs the whole of you.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A readiness low in the chest, like waiting for a door to open.\n- **The Belief.** This can carry a little more. *Go on, then.*\n- **The Impulse.** To take the next opening when it comes, and to make one if it is slow coming.',
        4: 'The exchange is inside the frame now, and inside it you say what you would not say outside it. Everything can be taken back — *only joking* — and so you can put more on the table than you could afford to mean, and you do. The check still runs: you know exactly what would land wrong, and you go up to that line and not over. Outwardly quick, close, a little dangerous to anyone who has not been let in. What would take this away is one of you stepping out of the frame; then whatever was last said counts.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Quickness. The next thing is ready before the last has finished.\n- **The Belief.** In here it costs nothing. *I can say that.*\n- **The Impulse.** To answer at once, and to answer with a little more than was given.',
        5: 'You are hunting openings now, and you push on the ones you find to see whether the frame holds. Every push is a test and you want the test to pass — that is the whole pleasure of it, that it holds. You choose who can take it and what they are safe in, and you would never push where it would break; if it broke, that would be the end of the game, and the game is the point. Outwardly you are a nuisance to exactly the people who can bear it. What would take this away is a push that lands as real.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A brightness in the eyes and hands; the body wants to be doing something a little wrong.\n- **The Belief.** It will hold. *Let me see if it holds.*\n- **The Impulse.** To press, lightly, at the place that is safest to press.',
        6: 'You have stopped checking outside. Whatever this is, it has the whole of your attention and you did not decide to give it; you notice, when something outside interrupts, that you had not been keeping watch for some time. Outwardly you are hard to reach, and slow to answer when reached. What would take this away is a break in the thing itself, not anything around it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The edges of the room have gone soft. Sounds arrive from far off.\n- **The Belief.** Nothing else is happening. *Just this.*\n- **The Impulse.** To stay in, and to resent, mildly, being pulled out.',
        7: 'The activity sets the pace now, not you. You do not decide when the next part comes; it comes, and you are there for it. There is still a you inside this, but it is a passenger — pleased, and not steering. Outwardly whatever the activity looks like from outside, done with a fluency that does not look decided. What would take this away is the activity ending, and for a moment after it ends you will not know what to do with your hands.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body is doing things ahead of the thought that would have chosen them.\n- **The Belief.** It is carrying me. *I am not the one doing this, quite.*\n- **The Impulse.** To keep up.',
        8: "The activity chooses, and you follow. What comes next is not offered to you for approval; it simply comes, and you are already doing it. Time has stopped being counted. Outwardly, if anyone is watching, you look as though you are not there — and in the sense that matters, you are not. What would take this away is nothing you would notice from inside it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Very little. The body is a thing that happens.\n- **The Belief.** There is no question. *Yes.*\n- **The Impulse.** Whatever the activity's next step is, already begun.",
        9: 'There is no gap between an opening and the act, and no one in the gap to choose. The thing is being done and there is no you standing beside it deciding to. Outwardly the same as the rung below, and only afterward, if it ends, will there have been anyone to say what it was like — and what they will say is that there was no outside to it. What would take this away cannot be said from here; there is nowhere else to say it from.\n\n- **The Sensation.** None reported. Afterward: as if surfacing.\n- **The Belief.** None. There is no room for one.\n- **The Impulse.** The act is the impulse; they are not two things.\n\n---',
    },
    'RECEPTIVITY': {
        1: 'You are not closed, and you did not open. Something could get in; nothing has yet. Outwardly nothing shows at all. What would take this away is the smallest refusal.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The face at rest. The breath unhurried and not held.\n- **The Belief.** Nothing is wrong with letting this near. *It can come if it comes.*\n- **The Impulse.** None yet.',
        2: 'Something has your leaning, and you did not decide to lean. Outwardly there is a slight turn toward it, easily missed. What would take this away is it turning out to be other than it seemed.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A small ease in the chest that arrived without a reason.\n- **The Belief.** This is on the better side of things. *I would not mind more of it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To stay turned toward it.',
        3: 'It has your attention, and you are taking note of why. Outwardly you are pausing to look longer than an ordinary glance. What would take this away is finding it had nothing more behind it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The gaze steadies. A mild settling in the breath.\n- **The Belief.** There is real quality here. *This is worth paying attention to.*\n- **The Impulse.** To stay with it a moment longer.',
        4: 'Your body took it up before you had any view about it. Outwardly something in you has loosened — the shoulders, the mouth — without instruction. What would take this away is it stopping.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Warmth where it touches. A softening you did not send for.\n- **The Belief.** This is good. *That is the whole of it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To let it go on.',
        5: 'It has coloured the rest. Things unconnected to it come easier than they did an hour ago. Outwardly you are lighter with everything, not only with it. What would take this away is the colour draining as ordinary things take their weight back.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A steady lift under the ribs that does not depend on where you look.\n- **The Belief.** Things are all right — more than the one thing. *It is a good day.*\n- **The Impulse.** To be easy with whatever comes next.',
        6: 'It does not fit your ordinary expectations, and your mind pauses trying to match it. Outwardly you have stopped still, your attention held fast, with no word ready. What would take this away is reducing it to something familiar.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A breath caught in suspension. An alert, open stillness through the chest.\n- **The Belief.** This exceeds what I knew how to look for. *I do not have a measure for this.*\n- **The Impulse.** To keep looking without trying to explain it.',
        7: 'You have gone small beside it, and being small is not the bad part. Outwardly you are very still and your gaze has gone long. What would take this away is it shrinking to your size.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The chest opens as though to take in more air than it needs. A slight unsteadiness, as if held up by less than usual.\n- **The Belief.** This is larger than me, and that is right. *I am allowed to be this small.*\n- **The Impulse.** To be quiet.',
        8: 'It has stopped you where you are. What you were doing is not resumed. Outwardly your hands have stopped mid-task and you have not noticed. What would take this away is the interrupted thing reasserting itself.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A brightness that arrives all at once, in the face and the hands. The breath catches upward.\n- **The Belief.** Nothing else matters for this moment. *This is the whole of now.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it, before any decision to move.',
        9: 'The thing has set your pace, and it is faster than everything around you. You cannot come down to meet it. Outwardly you are speaking and doing quicker than the room, and cannot slow when you try. What would take this away is the room catching up, or you running out.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Heat in the face, breath high and quick, a hum through the limbs that wants somewhere to go.\n- **The Belief.** Everything is possible right now. *There is no reason to hold back.*\n- **The Impulse.** To do everything at once.',
        10: 'It has taken over every channel of your attention; there is nothing left over to observe from or hold back with. Outwardly you are entirely swept up in it, unresponsive to whatever was happening around you. What would take this away is a shock violent enough to tear you out of it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A consuming fullness that fills the chest and ears like a tide, crowding out every other signal.\n- **The Belief.** This has claimed the whole of me. *There is nothing else to hold onto.*\n- **The Impulse.** To yield entirely to the sweep of it.',
        11: 'The boundary between you and what is happening has completely dissolved under absolute saturation. There is no distance left to measure from, and no capacity to register anything outside this. Outwardly you are lost to the world, unreachable, as if the room had ceased to exist. What would take this away is only the inevitable exhaustion of the state itself.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Total saturation. Every nerve carrying all it can bear, humming at full capacity.\n- **The Belief.** All of me is consumed by this. *There is no boundary left.*\n- **The Impulse.** None. Everything in you is already given over.\n\n---',
    },
    'SELF-REGARD': {
        1: 'You count, to yourself. At rest it costs nothing; you are simply someone, in your own accounting, and the accounting is not running now. Outwardly nothing. What would take this away is being made to feel you do not count.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Nothing. An ease of standing in your own skin.\n- **The Belief.** I am someone. *I count.*\n- **The Impulse.** None.',
        2: 'You have a standing, and you would notice it being touched. Nothing has touched it; the standing sits quiet, but there is a line around you now that was not marked before. Outwardly a certain bearing. What would take this away is the line being crossed without answer.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The spine a little longer. A stillness that is held, not slack.\n- **The Belief.** There is a way I am to be treated. *Not less than that.*\n- **The Impulse.** To keep the line.',
        3: 'Your standing is attached to something you did. It is not only that you count; you count because of that, and that is yours. Outwardly you carry it, and it shows when it comes up. What would take this away is the thing being taken from you, or shown to be less.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A lift in the chest when it is near. Warmth in the face.\n- **The Belief.** I did that. *That was me.*\n- **The Impulse.** To have it seen — or to hold it, if there is no one to see.',
        4: 'You weigh more than the business in front of you. Whatever the matter is, your part in it is the heavier part, and the matter arranges itself around your weight. Outwardly you take up the room — in time, in the order things go. What would take this away is the business turning out not to need you.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A settledness. Your own presence is the first fact in any room.\n- **The Belief.** This turns on me. *It matters because I am in it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To be at the centre of whatever is happening.',
        5: 'Your presentation must be maintained. How you appear — not only to others, to yourself — is a thing that needs tending, and it is being tended, always, under whatever else is happening. Outwardly you check, adjust, arrange. What would take this away is nothing that could see you, including you.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A low attention to your own surface. You are keeping an eye on it.\n- **The Belief.** I am seen, and what is seen must be right. *Let it hold.*\n- **The Impulse.** To adjust — to keep the surface as it should be.',
        6: "The estimate has stopped checking itself. What you think of yourself no longer asks the world for confirmation; it is settled, and the world's opinion, where it differs, is the world's mistake. Outwardly you do not ask, and are not corrected. What would take this away is a check you cannot dismiss.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A steadiness that nothing outside touches. No flicker when contradicted.\n- **The Belief.** I know what I am. *That is not in question.*\n- **The Impulse.** To carry on as you are, whatever is said.",
        7: 'Anyone there exists as audience or obstacle. They have force — they can watch, they can get in the way — but they do not have a standing of their own that weighs against yours. Outwardly you look past them or through them. What would take this away is someone whose standing you could not dismiss.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A looking-down that is not effortful. Others, when there are any, register at a distance.\n- **The Belief.** They are here for me, or in my way. *Which is it.*\n- **The Impulse.** Through them, or past them.',
        8: 'You are exempt from what binds everyone. The rules are real and they apply — to others. What would stop another person does not arrive at you as a stop. Outwardly you do what is not done, and it does not occur to you that it is not done. What would take this away is a cost you cannot step around.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A lightness where the weight of consequence should be. Nothing pushes back.\n- **The Belief.** That does not apply to me. *Not to me.*\n- **The Impulse.** To do it anyway — because the reason not to is for other people.',
        9: 'Others have no inside. They are not people with their own standing that you have dismissed; they are supply — what they give you is what they are, and when they stop giving they stop being anything. Outwardly you are warm when fed and cold the moment you are not; alone, there is only the cold. What would take this away is nothing anyone could do.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A hunger that is met by attention and by nothing else. When it stops, the hunger is what remains.\n- **The Belief.** They are what they give me. *Feed it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To draw from them.',
        10: 'The estimate has replaced the world. What is true is what agrees with your account of yourself; what does not agree is not happening. Outwardly you act on a world that is not there, and cannot be told. What would take this away is the world itself arriving — and it cannot get in.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Certainty with no edge to it. The body acts on what it expects, not what is there.\n- **The Belief.** It is as I say. *It is because I say.*\n- **The Impulse.** To enact the account.',
        11: "The estimate has replaced you. There is no person under it any more — no one who has the regard; the regard is what is standing there. Outwardly nothing is left that could be reached as a person. What would take this away is nothing that acts on a person.\n\n- **The Sensation.** There is no one to report from. The body is the estimate's.\n- **The Belief.** There is nothing to believe and no one to believe it. *I am what I am.*\n- **The Impulse.** None separate from the estimate. It does what it does.\n\n---",
    },
    'STIRRING': {
        1: 'Something has stirred. It has registered as more than the things around it, and that is all; nothing follows, and you could not say what it was about it. Outwardly nothing. What would take this away is it passing from view.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A slight lift of attention. Nothing in the body.\n- **The Belief.** There is something there. *That.*\n- **The Impulse.** None yet.',
        2: 'You want to know more of it. Not to have it — to find out what it is, how it works, what is behind it. Outwardly a question forms and may not be asked. What would take this away is knowing.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The head turns slightly. A small alertness at the front of the mind.\n- **The Belief.** I do not know enough about that yet. *What is it?*\n- **The Impulse.** To look closer.',
        3: 'You have turned toward it. Your attention has oriented and stays where it is; it costs nothing yet to keep it there, and nothing yet to look away. Outwardly you are facing it more than the room requires. What would take this away is something else taking the orientation.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body has squared slightly toward it. The eyes stay.\n- **The Belief.** That is where the interest is. *I have not looked away.*\n- **The Impulse.** To keep it in view.',
        4: 'You have started toward it. Orientation has become approach; whatever moving toward it means here, you are doing it, and you did not decide to. Outwardly you are moving toward it, in whatever way moving is available. What would take this away is it being reached, or it being gone.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A pull forward, low in the body. Something in you has already gone ahead.\n- **The Belief.** The want has your motion now. *Toward.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it — in whatever way toward is available.',
        5: 'It has spiked. For this moment the want is sharp and specific and wants acting on now — and if you wait, it will pass. Outwardly a sudden movement toward it, or a sudden stillness holding one back. What would take this away is the moment passing.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A jolt. Heat and a quickening, then it crests.\n- **The Belief.** Now. *Just this once, now.*\n- **The Impulse.** To reach — this instant, before it fades.',
        6: 'It no longer spikes; it runs. The want starts itself without a cue and does not wait to be prompted — and it can still be crowded out, for a while, by something louder. Outwardly you return to it between other things, every time. What would take this away is something strong enough to take its place.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A low steady pull that is there when you check for it. It is there when you do not.\n- **The Belief.** I want that. *Again, then.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it, and again, and again.',
        7: 'It does not pass. Waiting no longer works; the want holds at pitch and other things are done around it, not instead of it. Outwardly you are restless with anything that is not it. What would take this away is having it — nothing less.\n\n- **The Sensation.** An ache where the want sits. Restlessness in the hands.\n- **The Belief.** Nothing else will do. *It has to be that.*\n- **The Impulse.** To get it. Nothing else answers.',
        8: 'Your day has arranged itself around it. Not one want among your concerns — the shape of what you do and when has started to follow it. Outwardly your choices have a centre, and it is it. What would take this away is it being done, or gone.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A constant forward lean. Energy for it that you do not have for other things.\n- **The Belief.** This is what I am for right now. *Everything else can wait.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it, all day.',
        9: 'Its absence is what you feel now. Not the want of it — the lack; where it is not is a place in you, and you carry it. Outwardly you are somewhere else when you are not with it. What would take this away is it being there.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A hollow in the chest that has the shape of it. It aches when nothing is happening.\n- **The Belief.** Something is missing, and it is that. *Where it should be, there is nothing.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward where it is.',
        10: 'It has most of you. You can still turn to other things, but turning costs, and you turn back the moment the cost is paid. Outwardly you return to it, arrange for it, and can be pulled away only with effort. What would take this away is nothing you are willing to do.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A pressure behind everything else. The rest of the world is heard through it.\n- **The Belief.** There is nothing that matters like this does. *I cannot put it down.*\n- **The Impulse.** To have it. What is in the way has already stopped counting.',
        11: 'Refusal is not on offer. There is no version of the next moment in which you turn from it; the question of whether to pursue it does not arise. Outwardly everything you do is about it, whether or not it can be seen. What would take this away is having it — and having it might not.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body is pointed at it and does not point elsewhere. Nothing else registers as fully real.\n- **The Belief.** It is the only thing. *There is no not.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it. There is no other direction.\n\n---',
    },
    'WARINESS': {
        1: 'A small portion of your attention is held back from what you are doing and kept on watch. Nothing is actively going wrong and baseline safety remains in place, but you are no longer operating on complete trust. Outwardly, your pace and posture do not alter, and nobody looking at you would notice anything has changed. What ends this is the margin proving unnecessary.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Your breath is even, your pulse is calm, and only the corners of your perception remain lightly engaged.\n- **The Belief.** Things are proceeding as expected, but it costs nothing to keep an eye open. *It is sensible to keep something held in reserve.*\n- **The Impulse.** To continue with your work while keeping a fraction of your attention uncommitted.',
        2: 'A discordant note has entered your assessment of the situation. It has not attached to a definite fault or named hazard, but the comfortable neutrality of the room is gone. You continue what you were doing, yet every detail now carries a slight question mark beside it. What ends this is an unambiguous sign that everything is in order.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A faint, intermittent coolness at the back of the neck and a stillness in the chest that lingers after you exhale.\n- **The Belief.** Something here does not settle cleanly, even if I cannot yet point to it. *The ground is not quite as solid as it appeared.*\n- **The Impulse.** To look twice at whatever passes before you, without interrupting your outward routine.',
        3: 'A specific doubt has formed and will not dissolve under reassurances. You can see the point where the arrangement could fail, and setting the doubt aside requires an active effort of will that does not hold. Observers see you pausing or re-reading, but take you merely for someone being thorough. What ends this is inspecting the critical point and finding it sound.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A persistent knot just beneath the ribs that tightens whenever your attention drifts toward the decision.\n- **The Belief.** This arrangement carries a flaw that could turn against me. *I cannot simply trust this to turn out well.*\n- **The Impulse.** To test the weak spot, check the seams, and hesitate before committing further.',
        4: 'The possibility of an adverse turn has moved from a passing question into the center of your thinking. You find yourself constructing the sequence of how the trouble will unfold and what it will cost you if it lands. You are still performing your task, but your thoughts are increasingly occupied with hedging against the blow. What ends this is the source of danger being definitively averted or resolved.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Shallow respiration, a constant dryness on the tongue, and a cold tightness settling across the brow.\n- **The Belief.** An unfavourable turn is likely forming, and I will have to reckon with it soon. *I need to prepare for the landing.*\n- **The Impulse.** To construct contingencies, limit your commitments, and minimize your exposure before the turn arrives.',
        5: 'The prospect of trouble no longer waits for an invitation; it returns to your working memory on its own momentum every time you look away. You cannot keep your mind cleared for other tasks for more than a few moments before the liability reasserts itself. Outwardly, your expressions turn preoccupied and your answers are slightly delayed. What ends this is receiving conclusive proof that the threat has dissipated.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A dull, churning tension in the midsection and a repetitive twitch of alertness that resets without relief.\n- **The Belief.** Leaving this unattended leaves me vulnerable, and looking elsewhere does not make it safer. *It will not leave me alone.*\n- **The Impulse.** To circle the problem repeatedly, turning every angle over to find a way to make it safe.',
        6: 'The strain has crossed from your thinking into your physiology. Your body prepares for sudden action even while you attempt to remain still; muscles hold excess tension, breathing pattern is uneven, and small, involuntary movements begin to show in your fingers or feet. Anyone paying close attention can see that your composure is costing you effort to maintain. What ends this is the immediate tension breaking into unambiguous movement.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Tremor at the edges of the hands, a flutter in the stomach, and a cold sweat lining the palms.\n- **The Belief.** The situation is unstable and could break open at any second. *My body wants to jump before I tell it to.*\n- **The Impulse.** To shift position, fidget, discharge excess tension through minor motions, and brace for an abrupt shift.',
        7: 'Both your body and your thoughts are locked in a sustained, unrelenting siege of anticipation. The tension does not dip between thoughts; the physiology stays keyed high, draining your reserves and making ordinary concentration exhausting. You are caught in a continuous vigil against an impending crisis that offers no window of relief. What ends this is the situation resolving or exhaustion forcing a collapse of alertness.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A tight band across the chest that restricts full breaths, persistent coldness in the hands, and an elevated, thumping pulse.\n- **The Belief.** Something ruinous is hanging over me continuously, and there is no safe place to stand down. *I cannot let my guard drop for an instant.*\n- **The Impulse.** To scan restlessly for any sign of escalation, maintain total vigilance, and resist any invitation to relax.',
        8: 'Doubt and probability have given way to a heavy certainty: the blow is coming, and it will be severe. You have stopped weighing whether things will go wrong and have begun acting on the conviction that disaster is already on its way. Your movements become stiff, quiet, and deliberate, stripped of incidental leisure. What ends this is the anticipated blow striking or an undeniable contradiction of the premise.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A heavy, leaden sink in the guts, a chilling of the skin, and a quiet slowing of breath under overwhelming pressure.\n- **The Belief.** There is no longer any question of avoiding it; the ruin is on its way. *It is coming, and it will not miss.*\n- **The Impulse.** To batten down whatever can be protected, withdraw from vulnerable margins, and wait for the impact.',
        9: 'The impending harm has expanded to swallow the horizon; you can no longer conceive of an outcome where you emerge whole. The future presents itself not as a field of possibilities, but as a narrow corridor leading into inevitable ruin. Outwardly, your affect is drained, your gaze fixed, and initiative for any long-term project drops to zero. What ends this is the blow finally landing and the period of waiting ending.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A crushing cold in the chest cavity, limbs that feel heavy and unresponsive, and a dry, suffocating constriction in the throat.\n- **The Belief.** The catastrophe has already claimed the outcome; there is nowhere ahead that is safe. *Everything forward leads into the blow.*\n- **The Impulse.** To freeze in place, pull your limbs inward, and endure the interval until the impact hits.',
        10: 'An immediate, undeniable warning arrives and violently breaks your ongoing conduct. Whatever you were saying, writing, or holding is instantly abandoned; the hazard demands total and immediate redirection of your faculties. Outwardly, you freeze mid-sentence or drop your current task with sudden, unambiguous urgency that arrests everyone around you. What ends this is determining that the immediate warning was an error or neutralizing the point of contact.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A sharp, icy jolt through the spine, instantaneous dilation of the eyes, and a sharp, audible gasp that arrests the breath.\n- **The Belief.** The danger is here now, and continuing what I was doing is impossible. *Drop everything and look.*\n- **The Impulse.** To halt ongoing activity instantly, snap all focus directly to the hazard, and position for immediate defense.',
        11: 'The threat is immediately upon you and command over your own body is seized by autonomic recoil. The decision to flinch, cower, or recoil is not evaluated; the body pulls back from the hazard before conscious reasoning can intervene. Outwardly, you jump back, shield your vital centers, or shudder with stark physical revulsion. What ends this is the immediate physical proximity breaking or barriers intervening.\n\n- **The Sensation.** A violent lurch in the diaphragm, a flood of icy blood to the core, and an involuntary shudder racking the shoulders.\n- **The Belief.** It is right on top of me and I am unprotected. *Pull back before it hits!*\n- **The Impulse.** To flinch away, shield vulnerable areas with whatever is at hand, and pull back with desperate autonomic recoil.',
        12: 'Deliberate selection of action has broken down completely. The capacity to compare alternatives, weigh consequences, or evaluate trade-offs is gone; the organism executes frantic, uncalculated escape or thrashing without deliberation. Outwardly, your actions become erratic, disorganized, and desperate, knocking aside obstacles without regard for dignity or cost. What ends this is reaching physical safety or being forcefully restrained and grounded.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Suffocating shortness of breath, blood roaring in the ears, dizzying vertigo, and a desperate, burning compulsion to move.\n- **The Belief.** Staying here is immediate destruction; there is no time to choose how. *Out, out, get out now!*\n- **The Impulse.** To scramble, thrash, shove past any impediment, and break from the space with blind, disorganized desperation.',
        13: 'The last reserves of conscious agency are submerged by overwhelming, catastrophic extremity. You are completely engulfed; the boundary between yourself and the destroying reality has broken down, leaving only the raw, helpless agony of an organism facing obliteration. Outwardly, you are either locked in rigid, voiceless petrification or crying out in primal, unmodulated extremity. What ends this is surviving the climax of the event, physical exhaustion, or the extinction of consciousness.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Total sensory overload followed by tunneling darkness, icy paralysis through every limb, and complete numbness to pain.\n- **The Belief.** Destruction is total, absolute, and already here; nothing remains to be saved. *It is the end of everything.*\n- **The Impulse.** To collapse into total immobility, curl into the smallest possible space, or give way against the crushing darkness.\n\n---',
    },
}

# DESCENT_BLOCKS — the way down, above each path's pivot (src/engine/rungs.PIVOTS). Authored
# under '## The descent blocks' in docs/rungs/<PATH>.md; an empty dict is a path whose descent
# is not yet written (or, for GOODWILL and DISTASTE, has none by design). `rungs.block_for`
# serves one only when the caller says the mood is descending AND the rung is above the pivot.
DESCENT_BLOCKS = {
    'DEFLATION': {
        9: 'The pieces have started to hold, and what they hold is what happened. Nothing new arrives out of order; the old things are where the disorder left them, and they stay there now. Outwardly you are tracking what is around you again, and what you track is the wreck. What would take this away is the looking finishing, and it does not finish on your schedule.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body reports in order. The order is the wreck.\n- **The Belief.** It holds now. *I can see it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To look at it, and not touch.',
        10: 'There is someone in you again, and they are not yet anyone. The answer, when you are spoken to, has something behind it now, and it is not you yet. Outwardly you are present in a way you were not, and no one could say who. What would take this away is the someone becoming you, which is not done by wanting it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body notices itself being reported from. It is strange.\n- **The Belief.** There is someone here. *Not yet me.*\n- **The Impulse.** None the someone can name. To be where the body is.\n\n---',
    },
    'DISPLEASURE': {
        8: 'The wrong still stands as illegitimate and nothing has been conceded, but the demand for its repudiation is no longer being pressed. The ground you crossed to make it stays crossed; what has gone is the pushing. Outwardly you hold the position you took and do not advance it, and whoever is watching can see that the pressing has stopped without the standing changing. What ends this is time passing without the wrong being renewed.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The cold pressure in the chest without the surge behind it. The gaze unfixes; the hands are still.\n- **The Belief.** It had no right to exist and it still has none; I am no longer pressing that. *It stands. I am not pushing it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To keep the distance the repudiation opened, and to do nothing further across it.',
        9: 'Something has been broken, and the drive to break has consumed what it ran on. The demand to inflict damage is not being made now; what the body carries is that it was. Outwardly the hostility that was destructive has stopped moving and has not turned into anything else, and anyone near you can see the aftermath before they can see you. What ends this is rest — the nervous system refilling, hours not minutes.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Tremor in the hands and jaw, cold sweat, breath that comes fast and carries nothing. A deep ache in the muscles that did the breaking.\n- **The Belief.** The force went out of me into it, and there is not more to push with. *What is broken is broken.*\n- **The Impulse.** To lean on whatever will hold you up and wait for the shaking to stop.',
        10: "The widening has stopped. Everything the offense swallowed — what was attached to it, who was attached to it — stays swallowed; nothing is being added and nothing is being given back. Outwardly you are still where the reach stopped, and you are not turning toward anything. What ends this is the body's reserves returning, over hours, and the blunted senses coming back one at a time.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The roar receding to a ringing. Pain arriving now that was not registering before. A rigid pressure through the body with nothing left to drive it outward.\n- **The Belief.** Everything it took in, it took; I am not adding to it and I am not taking it back. *It reached where it reached.*\n- **The Impulse.** To stand where it stopped and not move toward anyone.",
        11: 'The mobilization has fallen below what it takes to lash out. The governor is not back; the fuel for continuous output is gone, and the stop signals that were not functioning — pain, exhaustion, resistance — are all arriving now, at once, late. Outwardly you have stopped in the middle of motion, and what is holding you up is not you. What ends this is sleep or unconsciousness.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Everything the exertion cost arriving together: the burn in every muscle, the injuries, the cold. Limbs too heavy to lift and not yet still.\n- **The Belief.** It stopped in me before it stopped around me. *There is nothing left to swing.*\n- **The Impulse.** To let whatever is holding you hold you, and not to stand.',
        12: 'The discharge has run out before the body failed. There is a witness again, dim and in pieces, arriving in a body it did not drive and does not yet answer to. Obstacles, living beings and objects are separating back into what they are, and the witness cannot yet say which of them it touched. Outwardly you are wherever the motion stopped, and you do not get up. What ends this is unconsciousness, being moved by others, or a long stillness.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Heaviness amounting to paralysis. The senses muffled and narrow. Breath ragged and shallow; muscles that do not answer.\n- **The Belief.** Fragments. The witness cannot yet say what it is witnessing. *—*\n- **The Impulse.** None the body can carry out. To lie where it stopped.\n\n\n---',
    },
    'DISTASTE': {},
    'GOODWILL': {},
    'LEVITY': {
        8: 'Time is being counted again, and the count says more went by than you have. What comes next is being offered for approval and you have no opinion about it. Outwardly you are there, if anyone is watching, and not yet doing anything with it. What would take this away is the room arriving — voices, the light, what is owed — and it arrives on its own.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Very little, still. The body is a thing that happened.\n- **The Belief.** It let go of me. *Where was I.*\n- **The Impulse.** To sit with it. Not to pick anything up yet.',
        9: 'There is someone beside the act again, and they arrived late: the thing was done, and now there is a you who was not there for it. The gap between an opening and the act is back, and no one is in it yet. Outwardly you have stopped, and only now is there anyone to say what it was like. What would take this away is the surfacing finishing, minutes, and it leaves you not knowing what to do with your hands.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body handed back. Hands that were doing something are yours, and idle.\n- **The Belief.** That happened, and I was not the one there. *I was in it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To go back under. It does not open.\n\n---',
    },
    'RECEPTIVITY': {
        10: 'The channels are being handed back one at a time. There is something left over to observe from now, and what it observes is the sweep, going out; you are still swept, and you know it. Outwardly you are coming back to whatever was happening around you, slowly, and not all the way. What ends this is the tide going all the way out, and it takes its own time.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The fullness in the chest and ears receding. Other signals arriving at the edges and not yet in the middle.\n- **The Belief.** This had the whole of me. *I can feel the edges of it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To yield to what is left of it, and not to reach for anything else yet.',
        11: 'The boundary is re-forming, and there is a you on this side of it again. What happened is still filling you and is no longer arriving; distance is possible, and you have not taken any. Outwardly you are reachable again and not yet reaching back, as if the room had only just resumed. What ends this is the fullness draining to where you can hold it — minutes to hours — and nothing hurries it.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Saturation, draining. Every nerve still humming, below what it can bear now.\n- **The Belief.** All of me was given to it. *I am still full of it.*\n- **The Impulse.** None. To stay where it left you.\n\n---',
    },
    'SELF-REGARD': {
        9: "The supply has stopped, and the hunger is what remains. Others still have no inside; they have stopped giving, and so there is only the cold. Outwardly you are cold with everyone, and it is not yet anyone's fault. What would take this away is the hunger dulling on its own, and it does not do so while anyone is watching.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The hunger, met by nothing. Cold where the warmth was being fed.\n- **The Belief.** They stopped giving. *They stopped being.*\n- **The Impulse.** To draw from them, and finding nothing, to withdraw.",
        10: 'The world is arriving and the account has not been corrected; both are in the room. What does not agree is happening now, and you act on the account anyway, with less of you behind it each time. Outwardly you enact a world that is not there and it does not answer, and you can be seen noticing. What would take this away is the account thinning, which it does by not being enacted, and you cannot stop enacting it yet.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Certainty with an edge to it now. The body acts on what it expects and meets what is there.\n- **The Belief.** It is as I said. *It is not answering.*\n- **The Impulse.** To enact the account, and to notice it not landing.',
        11: "There is a person under it again. Not yet one who has the regard — one the regard was standing on, arriving to find what it did. Outwardly something can be reached now that could not before, and it does not yet answer to a name. What would take this away is the person filling back in, and no one can be told to.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Someone to report from, and what is reported is the estimate's body: where it stood, what it did.\n- **The Belief.** Something was there that was not me. *I was it.*\n- **The Impulse.** None that is not the estimate's. Then one, small: to sit down.\n\n---",
    },
    'STIRRING': {
        8: 'The day is still arranged around it, and it has stopped arriving. The shape of what you do follows a centre that is not being fed; your choices keep their centre out of habit. Outwardly you go where you went for it, and stop. What would take this away is the day re-arranging, and it does not do so by being told.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The forward lean with nothing in front of it. The energy for it, unspent.\n- **The Belief.** This was what I was for. *What is it now.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it, and then stopping. All day.',
        9: 'The lack has stopped being carried toward anything. Where it is not is still a place in you; you are no longer going to it. Outwardly you are somewhere else, and there is no with-it to come back from. What would take this away is the hollow closing, and it closes from the edges, slowly.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The hollow in the chest, still the shape of it. It aches when nothing is happening, and nothing is happening.\n- **The Belief.** It is not where it should be, and I am not going. *Nothing there.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward nowhere. You stay.',
        10: 'It still has most of you, and it is not taking more. Turning to other things costs what it cost; you are not turning back, because there is nothing there to turn back to. Outwardly you arrange for it out of habit and stop halfway, and can be pulled away without the effort it used to take. What would take this away is the habit running out, days.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The pressure behind everything else, unchanged. The rest of the world heard through it, and nothing on the other side.\n- **The Belief.** It mattered like nothing else, and it still does. *I have put it down and my hands are still shaped for it.*\n- **The Impulse.** To arrange for it, and to stop.',
        11: 'Refusal is still not on offer, and nothing is asking. The pointing has nothing to point at that answers, and the body stays pointed. Outwardly everything you do is still about it, and none of it reaches anything. What would take this away is the pointing failing on its own, which takes longer than anyone around you can wait.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The body aimed at where it was. Nothing else registers as fully real yet.\n- **The Belief.** There is no not. *There is also no it, right now.*\n- **The Impulse.** Toward it. There is nowhere it is.\n\n---',
    },
    'WARINESS': {
        10: 'The warning has been looked at, and what you dropped is still where it fell. The redirection completed and nothing has taken its place; your faculties are pointed at where the hazard was and are not returning to what they were doing. Outwardly you are stopped mid-task with the task abandoned around you, and no new one has begun. What ends this is picking the dropped thing back up, which the body resists for a while before it allows.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The jolt gone and a hollow readiness in its place. Hands empty. The eyes still wide.\n- **The Belief.** I stopped everything for it, and I have not started anything since. *It was there.*\n- **The Impulse.** To look again at where the hazard was, rather than back at what you were doing.',
        11: 'The recoil has completed and the body is still braced in it. The flinch is held past the thing that caused it; the shield is up and nothing is pressing on it. Outwardly you are pulled back and covering, and you are not coming out. What ends this is the muscles releasing on their own — minutes, not seconds — and they do not take instruction.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Aftershocks of the shudder through the shoulders. The cold in the core ebbing. A brace that will not drop when told to.\n- **The Belief.** It did not hit, and the body has not been told. *Not yet.*\n- **The Impulse.** To keep whatever was shielding you where it is, and to check that the thing is not still there.',
        12: 'The scramble has stopped without deliberate selection coming back. You are wherever the last movement left you, and you do not know the sequence that put you there; what was knocked aside stays where it went. Outwardly you are still, and the stillness is not chosen either. What ends this is breath returning under your own count.\n\n- **The Sensation.** Breath still short without the compulsion behind it. Dizziness. Legs that will not take a plan.\n- **The Belief.** I stopped. I do not know what I did to get here. *Here.*\n- **The Impulse.** Not to move until moving is chosen again, and it is not yet.',
        13: "The engulfment is not being renewed. What remains is the organism the event went through: the boundary between you and it is re-forming, and what it encloses is shaking. There is a you again and it is very small. Outwardly you are locked in the posture the extremity left you in, and sound, if any, has gone out of you. What ends this is the body's exhaustion passing into sleep, or being held still by someone until it does.\n\n- **The Sensation.** The numbness giving way to cold. Hearing returning as a ring. Tremor in every limb, and no strength to stop it.\n- **The Belief.** It happened, and there is still someone here; that is as far as it goes. *Still here.*\n- **The Impulse.** To stay in the smallest space you can make, and not to be seen.\n\n---",
    },
}
